introduction:
Head-mounted cameras can record manipulation demonstrations without requiring an operator to teleoperate a robot. EgoMimic [2] co-trains on human and robot demonstrations, whereas EgoZero [3], Phantom [4] and HumanEgo [5] train manipulation policies from human demonstrations alone. We use HumanEgo as our reference. It represents a demonstration through hand trajectories, object keypoints and images in which a virtual gripper replaces the human arm. HumanEgo [5] reports training with approximately 30 minutes of human video per task.

EgoMimic [2], EgoZero [3] and HumanEgo [5] use Project Aria glasses [6]. In our Aria workflow, Meta’s Machine Perception Services (MPS) process uploaded recordings to estimate head and hand poses. HumanEgo [5] triangulates object keypoints from successive RGB views during an initial scene sweep, which can be a lateral head movement or a forward approach. We used a forward approach (the walk-up) for the matched-device comparison.

Consumer VR headsets offer a different profile: they track the head and both hands on the device, and several give applications access to their see-through cameras. The PICO 4 Ultra, which we use, has two such cameras 64 mm apart, and EgoKit [1] records both cameras’ images together with the head pose and 26-joint hand tracking. We present VREgo, an approach that learns robot manipulation policies from such recordings. It represents a demonstration as HumanEgo does, by the hand’s trajectory, the objects’ keypoints and an image without the arm, and learns the same kind of policy, so HumanEgo serves as the reference throughout. VREgo differs from it in four ways:

Capture device. A consumer VR headset, the PICO 4 Ultra, records the demonstrations and tracks the head and hands itself, and a stereo step locates the objects from its two cameras.

Data collection. Because stereo, not head motion, supplies the parallax, the wearer stands at the table from the first frame; there is no walk-up.

Pipeline. We adapted grasp labelling, object tracking and segmentation to a new operator and to recordings with two sequential moves.

Execution. For a two-move task, the training records are rewritten for a gripper, and rules for executing the policy’s output guide release timing, grasp position and placement.

We evaluate in simulation, using a MuJoCo arm, on two tasks recorded by one operator. Task 1, a cube onto a tray, was recorded on the same 41 layouts with the PICO and with Aria glasses, the reference’s device. Section 5.1 summarizes the device comparison also reported in the unpublished revision of the EgoKit manuscript [7]. Task 2, a block onto the tray and then the cube onto the block’s studs, was recorded with the PICO alone and without a walk-up. We examine two failure modes of the baseline and evaluate trajectory and execution changes designed to address them.

Our contributions are:

A capture path from a consumer VR headset, the PICO 4 Ultra, that reproduces all 864 training images in a format-conversion test on one Aria recording and which locates objects by stereo, so that a take needs neither a cloud service nor a walk-up;

A pipeline tuned to recordings from another device and operator, including two-move tasks, with each change measured on pilot takes, and fixes for defects in the reference implementation;

A simulated evaluation of closed-loop policy execution and replays every demonstration’s own hand path before any training, as a check on the data;

A collection of 123 demonstrations on two tasks and the policies trained on them, an analysis of why the two-move task fails, and changes to the training records and to the execution of the policy’s output that increase the proportion of episodes meeting both placement criteria from 2 to 93.8 % on the development layouts.

